[FINAL-TEXT] All evidence is in. Both VLAN subinterfaces active on sshswitch, traffic flowing, all five affected hosts reach their gateways successfully. The sshswitch startup explicitly documents the historical fault and fix. Writing final diagnosis.

=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
Thorough end-to-end investigation of the switch4 → sshswitch trunk and all five VLAN5/VLAN6 hosts found no active fault. All users on 10.5.0.x (pc1, pc2, dc_pc5) and 10.6.0.x (pc3, pc4) can reach their respective gateways (10.5.0.254 and 10.6.0.254) with zero packet loss, inter-VLAN routing is functional, and inter-campus connectivity to mswitch0 VLAN gateways (172.16.10.254, 172.16.20.254) works correctly.

The root cause of the originally reported symptom is documented directly inside the lab: sshswitch's startup script contains an explicit comment — "eth0 trunk to switch4 (was orphaned — translation bug)" — and explains that without VLAN5 and VLAN6 subinterfaces wired to the correct bridge instances on sshswitch's eth0 port, Campus-2 PCs in VLAN5/6 on switch4 could not reach their SVIs. The original translation of the Cisco running-config to Linux bridge commands failed to create eth0.5 (→ br_v5) and eth0.6 (→ br_v6) on the sshswitch eth0 uplink. With those missing, tagged frames arriving from switch4's trunk were invisible to the br_v5/br_v6 bridge domains that hold the gateway IPs, making 10.5.0.254 and 10.6.0.254 unreachable for all switch4 VLAN5/6 hosts simultaneously — precisely the "both segments broken" pattern in the ticket. The fault was on sshswitch, not switch4 as users suspected.

The corrective fix — adding eth0.5 → br_v5 and eth0.6 → br_v6 plus eth0 → br_v1 for native VLAN — has already been applied and is confirmed active in the running state (packet counters on both subinterfaces show live bidirectional traffic). No further action is required; the network is healthy.
=== END ===